FILE: app/src/jump-to-frame.ts
```ts
import { goToFrame, playAnimation } from "@babylonjs/lite";
import type { AnimationGroup } from "@babylonjs/lite";

/**
 * Jump a playing group to `frame` (in the clip's own frame rate) and keep it
 * playing from there, at its current speedRatio and loop setting.
 *
 * goToFrame converts the frame to seconds using the group's frame rate (60 if
 * none), moves currentTime, writes the pose, and pauses the group. We then call
 * playAnimation to resume from the new currentTime. goToFrame leaves
 * loopAnimation and speedRatio untouched, so those carry over.
 */
export function jumpToFrame(group: AnimationGroup, frame: number): void {
    // Property groups are owned by a manager (which may have an engine); glTF
    // groups played by the scene have no owner, so the engine stays undefined.
    const engine = group._animationManager?.engine;
    goToFrame(group, frame, engine);
    playAnimation(group);
}
```

I added `src/jump-to-frame.ts`. It calls `goToFrame`, which converts the frame to seconds at the clip's own frame rate and sets the time, then `playAnimation` to resume. Loop mode and `speedRatio` are left alone. Nothing was run, since the library isn't here.

I wasn't sure of three things:
- **Export:** I assumed `goToFrame` and `playAnimation` are exported from `@babylonjs/lite`. The notes don't list them as missing, but they don't confirm it either.
- **Property clips:** `goToFrame` clamps to the whole clip duration rather than the play range, so a frame outside `fromTime`/`toTime` is allowed. A non-looping clip jumped to its end will finish on the next update.
- **glTF clips:** I assumed `playAnimation` keeps the `currentTime` that `goToFrame` set.
